; `restrict(no-alloc)` against a CONSTRUCTOR, which is the form the
; check could not see until 2026-08-31.
;
; `no-alloc` reads the effect row, and a constructor application
; contributed nothing to that row - `findFnEnt` answers 0 for a `data`
; constructor and for a struct, and the branch that handled the answer
; added nothing. So a body whose only act was allocating a value
; checked clean under a claim that it allocates nothing.  Measured on
; `mk` below, against the compiler at 0.6.0:
;
;   $ axiom check 384-restrict-no-alloc-ctor.ax
;   OK
;   $ axiom emit-llvm --input 384-restrict-no-alloc-ctor.ax -o out.ll
;   $ awk '/^define .*@mk\(/,/^}/' out.ll | grep -c 'call i64 @axiom_alloc'
;   1
;
; The claim, its refutation and the compiler agreeing with the claim,
; in one file.  `docs/memory-model.md` `MM-EXEC-9a` had this listed as
; a DECISION rather than a gap; it is a decision no `restrict(no-alloc)`
; can survive, and the row is closed rather than documented now.
;
;   `mk`    matches on a `data` constructor it builds     -> AX3049
;   `pair`  builds a struct positionally                  -> AX3049
;   `kw`    builds the same struct, keyword spelling      -> AX3049
;   `tag`   answers a NULLARY constructor                 -> silent:
;           an immediate tag with no block behind it, and the emitted
;           body holds no `axiom_alloc` at all.  This arm is the half
;           that would go quiet under a fix that simply said "every
;           constructor allocates", so it is asserted, not assumed.
;   `some`  answers `(Some n)`                            -> AX3049
;   `none`  answers `None`                                -> silent
;   `pass`  arithmetic only                               -> silent
;
; `some` AND `none` ARE HERE BECAUSE `Option` IS WHERE A MIGRATION IS
; POINTED. `docs/error-model.md` ERR-REC-3 sends every ABSENCE sentinel
; - a lookup answering `-1` for "not found" - to `Option`, and §10.1
; reasoned that the modules dense with `restrict` claims were safe to
; migrate because they carry absence rather than failure, so they need
; no `Error` and no computed message. That is true and it is not the
; constraint: `(Some v)` is a constructor application like any other,
; and four of the ten absence sentinels left in `stdlib/` -
; `strHexVal`, `utf8DecodeAt`, `utf8CharAt` and `keyStrEnd` - carry
; `restrict(no-alloc)` themselves. They cannot become `Option` without
; withdrawing that claim.
;
; The pair is what makes it a measurement rather than a warning: `None`
; is silent, so the refusal is specifically about carrying a VALUE out
; of a lookup, and a partial migration that only ever answers `None`
; would not have found it.

(data W
  (Wrap Int)
  (Empty))

(struct P
  (a : Int)
  (b : Int))

;@axiom:restrict(no-alloc)
(:: mk (-> Int Int))

(fn (mk n) (match (Wrap n) ((Wrap x) x) ((Empty) 0)))

;@axiom:restrict(no-alloc)
(:: pair (-> Int P))

(fn (pair n) (P n n))

;@axiom:restrict(no-alloc)
(:: kw (-> Int P))

(fn (kw n) (struct P n n))

;@axiom:restrict(no-alloc)
(:: tag (-> Int W))

(fn (tag n) (Empty))

;@axiom:restrict(no-alloc)
(:: some (-> Int (Option Int)))

(fn (some n) (Some n))

;@axiom:restrict(no-alloc)
(:: none (-> Int (Option Int)))

(fn (none n) None)

;@axiom:restrict(no-alloc)
(:: pass (-> Int Int Int))

(fn (pass a b) (+ a b))

(:: sizeOf (-> W Int))

(fn (sizeOf w) (match w ((Wrap x) x) ((Empty) 0)))

(:: main Int)

(fn (main)
  (let (
    (p (pair 5))
    (q (kw 6))
  )
    (+ (mk 1) (+ (pass 2 3) (+ (sizeOf (tag 4)) (+ p.a (+ q.b
      (+ (match (some 7) ((Some x) x) (None 0))
         (match (none 8) ((Some x) x) (None 0))))))))
  )
)
